<Base title="Linked lists · ITC 120" description="Linked lists in plain English: nodes and references in Java, traversal, insertion, deletion, and singly, doubly, and circular lists." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Prelim › Linked lists</p>
    <p class="kicker">Linear data structures · Lesson 2 of 3</p>
    <h1>Linked lists</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">A chain of nodes. Nothing shifts: we change the arrows instead.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain how nodes and references form a linked list;</li>
        <li>trace access, traversal, insertion, and deletion on a box-and-arrow drawing and in Java;</li>
        <li>explain why the <b>order</b> of the lines matters when inserting;</li>
        <li>tell singly, doubly, and circular linked lists apart.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>Arrays lesson</a>, and scratch paper for drawing boxes and arrows.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="idea">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>A chain of nodes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The array's one real weakness: to insert 25 at index 2 of 10, 20, 30, 40, both 30 and 40 had to move. The more values after the gap, the more work. So what if the values did not have to sit side by side at all?</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llWeakPic()} /><figcaption>The array's weakness: every value after the gap moves.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Linked list</p>
        <p>A <b>linked list</b> is a <b>linear data structure</b> (a way to organize data where the items form one line, each item followed by at most one next item) made of a chain of <b>nodes</b>. A <b>node</b> is one small box of data in the chain. Each node stores two things: the value (<code>data</code>), and where to find the next node (<code>next</code>). A variable called <code>head</code> refers to the first node. The last node's <code>next</code> is <code>null</code>: the end. An <b>empty</b> list has <code>head</code> equal to <code>null</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llDefPic()} /><figcaption>head refers to the first node. Each node holds data and next. null marks the end.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Like a treasure hunt</p>
        <p>Each clue tells you where the next clue is hidden. To reach clue 4, you start at clue 1 and follow the clues in order. You cannot jump to clue 4. The first clue is like <code>head</code>, each hiding place is like <code>next</code>, and "this is the last clue" is like <code>null</code>.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llHuntPic()} /><figcaption>Follow the clues in order. You cannot skip to clue 4.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p>Here is the same sequence, organized two ways:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llTrainPic()} /><figcaption>A seat plan jumps straight to a seat. A train is walked car by car.</figcaption></figure>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Array</th><th>Linked list</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Numbered slots, side by side.</td><td>Nodes can be anywhere in memory.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The <b>index</b> tells you where to go.</td><td>The <b>previous node</b> tells you where to go next.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reach any slot in one step.</td><td>Walk from <code>head</code>, one link at a time.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insert or delete: shift values.</td><td>Insert or delete: change links.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Java first</p>
      <h2>Nodes and references in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>A <b>class</b> is a blueprint. An <b>object</b> is one real thing built from it. This class describes one node:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(node)} /></pre>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>int data;</code></td><td>A <b>field</b> that holds the node's value.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Node next;</code></td><td>A field that holds a <b>reference</b> to another node, or <code>null</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Node(int value)</code></td><td>The <b>constructor</b>: same name as the class, no return type. It runs once, automatically, each time <code>new Node(...)</code> runs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p><code>Node first = new Node(10);</code> does three things: <code>new</code> creates a node in memory, the constructor stores 10 in <code>data</code> and <code>null</code> in <code>next</code>, and <code>=</code> stores a reference to that node in the variable <code>first</code>. (The name <code>first</code> is our choice, not a Java keyword.)</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llNewPic()} /><figcaption>One line, three jobs.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Reference</p>
        <p>A <b>reference</b> tells Java <b>where to find</b> an object. It is not the object itself. The special value <code>null</code> refers to no object.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Assignment copies the reference, not the node</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(same)} /></pre>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llNamesPic()} /><figcaption>Same node, two names vs. two separate nodes. The shirt number is the data.</figcaption></figure>
        <p><code>Node b = a;</code> does <b>not</b> make a new node. It gives the same node a second name. Like a classmate with two names, Migs and Miguel: if Migs gets a new shirt number, Miguel has it too, because they are the same person. Compare <code>Node b = new Node(5);</code>, which makes a separate node: changing <code>b.data</code> would not touch <code>a.data</code>.</p>
        <p>The dot in <code>b.data</code> means "the <code>data</code> field of the node that <code>b</code> refers to."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Connecting two nodes</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(link)} /></pre>
        <div class="mini-ll" style="margin-block:1.6rem" aria-label="ten refers to node 10, which points to node 20, which points to null"><b>10</b>→<b>20</b>→<i>null</i></div>
        <p><code>ten.next</code> now stores a reference to node 20, not the number 20. The number is <code>ten.next.data</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">NullPointerException</p>
        <p>An <b>exception</b> is an error Java reports while the program is running. If a reference is <code>null</code>, using <code>.data</code> or <code>.next</code> on it throws (reports) a <b>NullPointerException</b>, and the program stops at that line. Before writing <code>x.next</code>, ask: can <code>x</code> be <code>null</code> here?</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="traverse">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Operations 1 and 2</p>
      <h2>Accessing and traversing a linked list</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Access and traversal</p>
        <p><b>Access</b> means getting one item. <b>Traversal</b> means visiting every item, one by one, in order. You met both in the Arrays lesson. In a linked list, both start at <code>head</code> and follow the <code>next</code> links.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llTravPic()} /><figcaption>Traversal visits every node, then stops at null.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>This code builds the list 10 → 20 → 30 and prints it. We keep the name <code>twenty</code> for node 20, so later steps can use it without searching.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(build)} /></pre>
      <p>Here is <code>printList</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(print)} /></pre>
      <p>When <code>main</code> calls <code>printList(head)</code>, <code>start</code> gets a copy of head's reference. Then <code>current</code> copies it again. All three refer to node 10, but they are separate variables. <code>current = current.next;</code> moves only <code>current</code>. It does not move any node, and it does not change <code>head</code>.</p>
      <p>Two words in the first line: <code>static</code> lets <code>main</code> call <code>printList</code> directly, without making an object first, and <code>void</code> means it sends no value back (it only prints). Also, each method has its own variables: the <code>current</code> inside <code>printList</code> is separate from the <code>current</code> that <code>main</code> declares later in the whole program, even though the names match.</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>current</th><th>This step prints</th><th>Then</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>10</td><td><code>10 -&gt; </code></td><td>move to 20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>20</td><td><code>20 -&gt; </code></td><td>move to 30</td></tr>
            <tr><td>30</td><td><code>30 -&gt; </code></td><td>30's next is null, so current becomes null</td></tr>
            <tr><td>null</td><td>(the loop stops)</td><td>print <code>null</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Output: <code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null</code></p>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Forgetting <code>current = current.next;</code></p>
        <p>Without that line, <code>current</code> never moves. It stays on node 10, so <code>current != null</code> is true forever: the loop prints <code>10 -&gt; </code> again and again and never ends.</p>
      </div>
      <CodeWalk id="cw-ll-stuck" code={llStuckCode} steps={llStuckSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: the loop without the move line" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">null, two ways</p>
        <p>In <code>current != null</code>, <code>null</code> is a <b>reference value</b>: "no node." In <code>System.out.println("null")</code>, it is just <b>text</b> in quotation marks that the program prints.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llNullPic()} /><figcaption>Same word, two jobs.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      \1
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llAccessPic()} /><figcaption>Access stops at the node it needs.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="lab">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Watch it</p>
      <h2>The linked list lab</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Every operation in this lesson, one line of Java at a time. Each button starts from a short list, so you can replay any of them. Read Parts 5 and 6 below, then come back and try each one.</p>
      <LinkedLab id="lab-ll" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="insert">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Operation 3</p>
      <h2>Insertion: change links, not values</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Insert at the beginning</h3>
        <p>Start with 10 → 20 → 30. Insert 5 before 10:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-ll-front" code={llFrontCode} steps={llFrontSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: insert 5 at the beginning" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
        <p>Nothing shifted. Two references changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Swap the lines, and the list is lost</p>
        <p>If <code>head = newNode;</code> runs first, then <code>newNode.next = head;</code> copies the <b>new</b> head, so node 5 refers to itself. Nothing refers to node 10 anymore, and <code>printList(head)</code> prints 5 forever.</p>
      </div>
      <CodeWalk id="cw-ll-swap" code={llSwapCode} steps={llSwapSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: the same lines, swapped" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Insert after a node you already have</h3>
        <p>Start with 10 → 20 → 30, and <code>current</code> refers to node 20. Insert 25 after it:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-ll-after" code={llAfterCode} steps={llAfterSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: insert 25 after node 20" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
        <p><code>current.next</code> is the reference stored in node 20, which refers to node 30. So line 3 makes 25 refer to 30. Then line 4 makes 20 refer to 25. Result: 10 → 20 → <b>25</b> → 30.</p>
        <p class="small muted">Here we already had a reference to node 20. To insert after a <b>value</b>, you would first traverse to find that node.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">The safe order for inserting</p>
        <ol>
          <li>First, make the new node refer to the node that will follow it.</li>
          <li>Then, change the old reference to point to the new node.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If B is the only thing pointing to C, and you change B first, nothing points to C anymore. The rest of the list is unreachable. Try "Insert: wrong order!" in the lab to see it happen.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llBarsPic()} /><figcaption>Grab the next bar before you let go.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="delete">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Operation 4</p>
      <h2>Deletion: link around the node</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Delete the node after another one</h3>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llTrainSkipPic()} /><figcaption>Deleting is like unhooking a train car.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>Start with 10 → 20 → 25 → 30. To delete 25, we need a reference to the node <b>before</b> it, because a singly linked list cannot go backward. <code>previous</code> refers to node 20:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-ll-bypass" code={llBypassCode} steps={llBypassSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: delete the node after 20" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
        <p>Read it piece by piece: <code>previous.next</code> is node 25. <code>previous.next.next</code> is node 25's next, which is node 30. Storing that in <code>previous.next</code> makes 20 point to 30. Node 25 is no longer reachable from <code>head</code>, so it is no longer in the list. Nothing else moves.</p>
        <p class="small muted">Is node 25 gone? It is out of the <b>list</b>. Once nothing at all refers to a node, Java's <b>garbage collector</b> (the part of Java that cleans up unused objects) frees its memory later. You do not delete it yourself. (In the whole program below, the variable <code>newNode</code> still refers to node 25, so that node still exists for now.)</p>
        <p class="small muted">This line needs <code>previous != null</code> and <code>previous.next != null</code>. Otherwise it uses a null reference and throws a NullPointerException.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Delete the first node</h3>
        <p>Deleting the first node only moves <code>head</code>:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(first)} /></pre>
        <p>5 → 10 → 20 → 30 becomes 10 → 20 → 30. This needs <code>head != null</code> (a list that is not empty). If the list had only one node, <code>head</code> becomes <code>null</code>: the list is now empty.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The whole program</h3>
        <p>Here is everything together. The program runs all four operations one after another on <b>one</b> list, so its lists are a little longer than the short examples above. Run it and match each output line to its <code>printList</code> call.</p>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the complete program</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(full)} /></pre>
          <TryIt />
          <pre class="code"><code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null
5 -&gt; 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null
5 -&gt; 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 25 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null
5 -&gt; 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null
10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30 -&gt; null</code></pre>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The real cost</h3>
        <p>Changing links is cheap. <b>Finding</b> the node is not:</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llStreetPic()} /><figcaption>House numbers vs. directions.</figcaption></figure>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Array</th><th>Linked list</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Get the value at index 500 (the 501st item)</td><td>1 step</td><td>start at <code>head</code> and follow 500 links</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Insert at a spot you already reached</td><td>shift every later value</td><td>change 2 links</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Delete the last value</td><td>just reduce the size</td><td>walk to the node before it</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="remember"><p class="note-label">Not always faster</p><p>Linked lists edit faster, once you are there. Arrays reach faster. Pick the one that fits what the program does most.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="kinds">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Three kinds</p>
      <h2>Singly, doubly, and circular</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llRoadsPic()} /><figcaption>Three kinds, three kinds of road.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="ds-grid">
        <div class="ds-card"><p class="ds-k">Singly linked</p>
          <p>Each node knows only what comes <b>next</b>. It moves forward only. To reach the node before B, start over from the head. <b>Like checking attendance</b> from the top of the class list to the bottom.</p></div>
        <div class="ds-card"><p class="ds-k">Doubly linked</p>
          <p>Each node also has a <b>prev</b> link, so it moves both ways. The cost: more memory, and more links to update on every change. <b>Like a photo viewer</b> with Next and Previous buttons.</p></div>
        <div class="ds-card"><p class="ds-k">Circular</p>
          <p>The last node points back to the first. There is no <code>null</code> at the end, so a loop must stop when it gets back to where it started. <b>Like a cleaning duty schedule</b> that returns to the first name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llKindsPic()} /><figcaption>The same three kinds as nodes and arrows.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Here they are side by side:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Singly</th><th>Doubly</th><th>Circular</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Links per node</b></td><td>next</td><td>prev and next</td><td>next, or both</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Move backward?</b></td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>only if doubly</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>End of the list</b></td><td>last next is null</td><td>null at both ends</td><td>loops back to the first</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Remove a node you have</b></td><td>need the node before it</td><td>directly, using prev</td><td>depends on singly or doubly</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Trade-off</b></td><td>one direction only</td><td>more memory and updates</td><td>needs a stop rule</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Circular is not a third number of links</p>
        <p>"Circular" describes the <b>loop</b>, not how many links each node has. A circular list can be singly or doubly linked. A playlist on repeat with Next and Previous is <b>circular doubly linked</b>.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={llCircPic()} /><figcaption>Circular singly and circular doubly: both loop back.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 8 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Practice on paper</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Draw boxes and arrows for every problem. That is what you will do on the exam. The first two sets are the same as the "Check your understanding" items in the module (Sections 3 and 4), so you may have done them already. The third set is new.</p>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Nodes and references</p>
        <ol>
          <li>What does this print? Explain why.<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(q1)} /></pre></li>
          <li>After these lines, how many Node objects exist? What is <code>first.next.data</code>?<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(q2)} /></pre></li>
          <li>What happens when <code>Node n = null;</code> is followed by <code>System.out.println(n.data);</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
          <ol>
            <li>It prints <b>3</b>. <code>new</code> ran twice, so <code>p</code> and <code>q</code> refer to different nodes. Changing <code>q.data</code> does not affect <code>p</code>.</li>
            <li><b>Two</b> nodes. <code>third</code> is just another reference to the second node. <code>first.next.data</code> is <b>2</b>.</li>
            <li>A <b>NullPointerException</b>: <code>n</code> refers to no node. Nothing catches it here, so the program stops at that line.</li>
          </ol>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Insert and delete</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Start with head → 10 → 20 → 30 → null. Draw the list after these three lines. Then draw it again after <code>head = head.next;</code>.<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(q4)} /></pre></li>
          <li>Explain in your own words: <code>newNode.next = current.next;</code></li>
          <li>In head → 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → null, <code>previous</code> refers to node 20. Draw the list after <code>previous.next = previous.next.next;</code>. Which node is no longer reachable?</li>
          <li><b>Debugging.</b> The list is head → 10 → 20 → 30 → null, <code>current</code> refers to node 20, and <code>newNode</code> refers to a new node 25 (its next is null). A student wrote these lines to insert 25 after 20. Draw the result. What goes wrong?<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(bug)} /></pre></li>
          <li>Name the kind of linked list: (a) nodes have prev and next, and both ends hold null; (b) the last node's next refers to the first, and nodes have no prev; (c) a playlist on repeat with Next and Previous buttons.</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
          <ol>
            <li>After the three lines: head → 10 → <b>15</b> → 20 → 30 → null (15 goes after the node head refers to). After <code>head = head.next;</code>: head → 15 → 20 → 30 → null.</li>
            <li>The new node now refers to the node that comes after <code>current</code>, so the rest of the list stays reachable before <code>current</code> is changed.</li>
            <li>head → 10 → 20 → 40 → null. Node <b>30</b> is no longer reachable from head.</li>
            <li>After <code>current.next = newNode;</code>, nothing refers to 30: it is lost. Then <code>newNode.next = current.next;</code> copies a reference to 25 itself, so 25 points to 25. The list reads 10 → 20 → 25 → 25 → …, and a traversal prints 25 forever.</li>
            <li>(a) Doubly linked. (b) Circular singly linked. (c) Circular doubly linked: it repeats (circular) and goes back (prev).</li>
          </ol>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">New problems</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The list is head → 4 → 9 → 16 → 25 → null. Write the three values printed, one per line. After the code, which node does <code>head</code> refer to?<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(n1)} /></pre></li>
          <li>The list is head → 7 → 14 → null. Draw the list after these lines. Which line runs "connect the new node first"?<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(n2)} /></pre></li>
          <li>The list is head → 2 → 4 → 6 → 8 → null. Draw the list after these lines. Which nodes are no longer reachable from head?<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(n3)} /></pre></li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
          <ol>
            <li><b>16</b>, <b>25</b>, <b>9</b>. <code>current</code> starts at 9 (<code>head.next</code>), then moves to 16. <code>current.next.data</code> is 25, and <code>head.next.data</code> is still 9. <code>head</code> still refers to node <b>4</b>: only <code>current</code> moved.</li>
            <li>head → 3 → 7 → <b>10</b> → 14 → null. Lines 2-3 insert 3 at the beginning (new node first, then <code>head</code>). Line 5, <code>b.next = head.next.next;</code>, connects the new node first: <code>head.next</code> is 7, so <code>head.next.next</code> is 14. Then line 6 makes 7 refer to 10.</li>
            <li>head → 4 → 6 → null. The first line drops node 2. <code>prev</code> refers to 6, and <code>prev.next.next</code> is 8's next, which is <code>null</code>, so 6 now ends the list. Nodes <b>2</b> and <b>8</b> are no longer reachable from head. (This works because <code>prev.next</code> is not null.)</li>
          </ol>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

